{{Politics of Ukraine}}

Early [[Ukrainian presidential elections|presidential elections]] were held in [[Ukraine]] on 26 June 1994, with a second round on 10 July.<ref name=NS>[[Dieter Nohlen]] & Philip Stöver (2010) ''Elections in Europe: A data handbook'', p1976 {{ISBN|9783832956097}}</ref><ref>{{Cite journal |last=Birch |first=Sarah |date=1995 |title=The Ukrainian parliamentary and presidential elections of 1994 |url=http://dx.doi.org/10.1016/0261-3794(95)95775-6 |journal=Electoral Studies |volume=14 |issue=1 |pages=93–99 |doi=10.1016/0261-3794(95)95775-6 |issn=0261-3794|url-access=subscription }}</ref> They were held ahead of schedule following a compromise between President [[Leonid Kravchuk]] and the [[Verkhovna Rada]], the Ukrainian parliament. The elections saw Kravchuk defeated by his former [[Prime Minister of Ukraine|Prime Minister]] [[Leonid Kuchma]]. They were the first presidential elections in the [[Commonwealth of Independent States]] in which the incumbent was defeated.

Kuchma took office on 19 July, marking the first [[peaceful transition of power|peaceful transfer of power]] in Ukraine since the fall of Communism.

==Background==
On 17 June 1993, the Verkhovna Rada voted to hold a referendum on 26 September that would serve as a [[motion of no confidence]] in President Kravchuk. However, the referendum was cancelled two days before it was due to be held. The [[Verkhovna Rada]] instead decided to hold [[1994 Ukrainian parliamentary election|early parliamentary elections]] on 24 March 1994, and early presidential elections two months later.

==Results==
In the first round, Kravchuk was supported by the [[People's Movement of Ukraine|People's Movement]] (which had originally collected signatures for [[Volodymyr Lanovyi]]) and the Democratic Association, an alliance of right-wing parties that viewed Kuchma as pro-Russian. Kuchma was supported by the Interregional Bloc of Reforms, and [[Socialist Party of Ukraine|Socialist Party]] candidate [[Oleksandr Moroz]] was supported by the [[Communist Party of Ukraine|Communist Party]] and [[Peasant Party of Ukraine|Peasant Party]].<ref name=political_parties>[https://web.archive.org/web/20140923004702/http://analitik.org.ua/ukr/publications/joint/3dd12dea/3dd13f15/ Political parties of Ukraine in cooperation with structures of power] Kyiv center of political research and conflictology</ref> After Moroz was knocked out, the Communists supported Kuchma in the second round.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Polishchuk |first=Tatiana |date=1999-05-15 |title= |script-title=uk:Вибори-99: Кучма і КПУ — знову разом! |trans-title=Elections-99: Kuchma and CPU – together again! |url=https://day.kyiv.ua/article/podrobytsi/vybory-99-kuchma-i-kpu-znovu-razom |access-date=2023-07-10 |website=day.kyiv.ua |language=uk}}</ref>

